    "body": "Miami-based cargo airline Amerijet International plans to repair a 36-year-old Boeing 767-300 converted freighter aircraft that suffered fuselage damage from a hard landing in Guatemala City last month, CEO Joe Mozzali said.\nThe aircraft’s age left many speculating that it wasn’t worth fixing and would be scrapped for parts, but Mozzali said Amerijet’s insurance company will cover the cost of the repair because it is less expensive than paying a claim for an aircraft declared totally damaged.\nIn reality, even badly damaged freighter aircraft are too valuable to part with because of the current shortage of widebody freighters, supply chain challenges that are slowing passenger-to-freighter conversions and limited feedstock caused by passenger airlines holding longer onto aircraft because engine-quality problems are slowing delivery of new aircraft, according to air cargo experts.\nAmerijet has hired a local repair station to conduct a temporary repair in Guatemala City and will then ferry the aircraft to a maintenance facility for a longer-term repair, Mozzali told FreightWaves in an email exchange. It is uncertain when the plane will be ready to return to service.\nThe Amerijet aircraft departed Miami on Sept. 6 and landed hard on its nose gear first, resulting in buckle damage to the top of the fuselage, according to The Aviation Herald and photos on social media. The aircraft has remained grounded at Guatemala City International Airport since the incident.\n✈️ Amerijet 767 Suffers Fuselage Damage in Guatemala City Amerijet Boeing 767-300F N373CM sustained visible upper-fuselage buckle damage after a hard landing at Guatemala City (GUA) on September 6, 2026. The aircraft remains AOG with damage recorded around STA 650, while photos… pic.twitter.com/wPemFedsHR — aircraftmaintenancengineer (@airmainengineer) September 12, 2026\nAviation safety experts say “skin buckling” can occur when the structure beneath the skin, including ribs and load-bearing beams, is distorted or bent. The stress from a hard landing could transfer through the landing gear to the landing gear fittings to the wing, and then carry through to the fuselage structure.\nThe damaged 786-300 was built for Qantas Airlines in November 1990. The plane is owned by Cargo Aircraft Management, the leasing arm of Wilmington, Ohio-based Air Transport Services Group, which converted it to carry main deck cargo in 2016.\nAmerijet has 11 B767-300s in its fleet. Mozzali said N373CM has been operating as a spare and being out of service hasn’t impacted Amerijet’s scheduled transportation or charter services. Amerijet is operating two freighters for United Parcel Service to help the parcel logistics giant with extra demand during peak shipping season, which runs until late December.\nClick here for more FreightWaves/American Shipper stories by Eric Kulisch.\nWrite to Eric Kulisch at ekulisch@freightwaves.com .\nDavid Bassett, founder of all-cargo airline Amerijet, dies at 77\nMaersk Air Cargo to cancel Asia-US flying with Boeing 767s, Amerijet\nUpcoming FreightWaves Events\nCompliance\nBrokerage Compliance Symposium\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nAwards\nF3 Awards Dinner\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nFreightTech\nF3: Future of Freight Festival\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nOctober 27, 2026 – October 28, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nCompliance Brokerage Compliance Symposium Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nAwards F3 Awards Dinner Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nFreightTech F3: Future of Freight Festival Oct 27 – Oct 28 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now",
